<p>I've been using LaTeX to generate PDF documents for a long time. I like the way LaTeX does typography and layout. Its output looks &quot;correct&quot; to me. That's probably because LaTeX tries hard to do the right thing. I've never seen output from a word processor that looks nearly as good.</p>
<p>I don't write raw LaTeX. I use it as a device for converting Org mode or Markdown files to PDF, usually via Pandoc.. I've built a complex workflow around it. Scripts, templates, boilerplate, etc. It works well. It's also wildly complicated. And so, enter <a href="https://typst.app/">Typst</a>.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>People have been mentioning Typst as a modern LaTeX alternative. I ignored it because I'd already solved the problem. It turns out that dismissing Typst as &quot;LaTeX for Losers&quot; was a mistake. I finally gave it a go, and found that it's similar to LaTeX, but without the steep learning curve, complexity, or multi-gigabyte download. The output is slightly different, but still looks great.</p>
<p>I've updated my scripts and various utilities to use Typst. It's open source, distributed as a single binary. And it's so fast!</p>
<p>There's some residual guilt for leaving LaTeX after so many years. I think it'll be OK without me.</p>
